# Tier D exclusions — psql constructs deliberately NOT in the manifest.
#
# Two kinds of entry live here, and each must say which it is in its reason:
#   1. Constructs the library does not render yet: the reason says what the
#      construct is and that it is planned, so the exclusion is auditable debt.
#   2. Grammar defaults that are deliberately unrepresentable: writing them
#      adds nothing to the SQL, and the library's design says the absence of
#      the clause is how the default is spelled.
#
# Moving an entry from here into psql.manifest is how new rendering work
# becomes gated.

[select.all]
reason = SELECT ALL is the grammar default; deliberately unrepresentable, the absence of DISTINCT is what it means (see keelson-psql extras::Distinct)

[group_by.all]
reason = GROUP BY ALL is the grammar default; deliberately unrepresentable (see shared::group_by_distinct)

[combine.union_distinct]
reason = UNION DISTINCT is the grammar default; deliberately unrepresentable, shared::union renders the bare UNION that means the same thing

[frame.from_unbounded_following]
reason = representable (the grammar's frame_start production lists UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING) but unjudgeable: PostgreSQL's gram.y raises "frame start cannot be UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING" while building the parse tree, so no judged corpus can contain it; pinned by the_frame_bounds_the_grammar_itself_refuses in keelson-psql/tests/grammar_select.rs

[frame.to_unbounded_preceding]
reason = representable (frame_end lists UNBOUNDED PRECEDING) but unjudgeable: gram.y raises "frame end cannot be UNBOUNDED PRECEDING" at parse time; pinned by the_frame_bounds_the_grammar_itself_refuses in keelson-psql/tests/grammar_select.rs

[expr.group]
reason = plain parenthesisation (psql::group with one element) is not a parse-tree construct — PostgreSQL discards the parentheses — and it is exercised by every chained operator in the corpus
